; BBC BASIC (BASIC 2, the BBC B's) seen from the assembler: the shape of a
; tokenised program, and the workspace BASIC shares with the machine code it
; CALLs. Used by MENU and MISSION.

; A program is a run of line records, ended by &0D &FF:
;
;   &0D, line number (high byte first), record length (counting these four
;   header bytes), then the tokenised text
;
; Keywords are single bytes from &80 (REM is &F4); a line number after
; GOTO, GOSUB, RESTORE or THEN is &8D and three bytes; REM and DATA, strings
; and `*` commands are stored as typed. Baron's BASIC...ENDBASIC block
; tokenises source text into this itself. basic_line() is for a line the
; block can't take as source text: one carrying bytes a source line can't
; hold (a newline) or that would make the file invalid UTF-8 (top-bit-set
; bytes). ENDBASIC always ends the program, so such lines can only come
; before the BASIC block.
FUNCTION basic_line(number, text) = CONCAT({&0D, HI(number), LO(number), LEN(text) + 4}, CODES(text))

BASIC_TOKEN_REM = &F4

; The resident integer variables @% and A% to Z%: four bytes each, low byte
; first, from &0400 in alphabetical order (@% first). They survive RUN and
; CHAIN, and CALL passes A%, X% and Y% in A, X and Y.
BASIC_RESIDENT_INTEGERS = &0400
FUNCTION basic_integer(letter) = BASIC_RESIDENT_INTEGERS + 4 * (CODES(letter)[0] - CODES("@")[0])

; Zero page BASIC leaves to the user, and to the machine code it CALLs.
BASIC_USER_ZP = &70     ; to &8F

; The programs hide things from LIST with VDU codes (VDU_DISABLE, VDU_ENABLE
; and VDU_DELETE, in osconst.6502inc): LIST copies a string's bytes (and a
; REM's, once a quote has started one) straight to the screen.
